              "y_percent": 22.0,
              "w_percent": 40.0,
              "h_percent": 32.0,
              "popover_title": "Development Aspirations",
              "popover_content": "Material vs Non-Material goals. Example: Dam construction creating power for industrialists while displacing indigenous riverine tribes."
            },
            {
              "id": "h1_2",
              "x_percent": 55.0,
              "y_percent": 22.0,
              "w_percent": 40.0,
              "h_percent": 32.0,
              "popover_title": "World Bank vs UNDP Metrics",
              "popover_content": "Per Capita Income = Total Income / Population. World Bank threshold: Rich > $49,300 vs Low <= $2,500. UNDP HDI incorporates health and education."
            }
          ]
        },
        "key_terms": [
          "Per Capita Income",
          "Human Development Index",
          "Infant Mortality Rate",
          "Literacy Rate",
          "Net Attendance Ratio",
          "Body Mass Index",
          "Sustainable Development"
        ],
        "key_takeaways": [
          "Development requires income plus non-material dignity, safety, and freedom.",
          "Average income hides wealth inequality.",
          "Public facilities are essential for human capital development.",
          "Resources must be managed sustainably to protect future generations."
        ]
      },
      {
        "chapter_number": 2,
        "chapter_title": "Sectors of the Indian Economy",
        "executive_summary": "Economic activities are classified into Primary, Secondary, and Tertiary sectors. In India, while Tertiary GDP share grew to over 50%, agricultural labor remained stuck at over 44%, creating disguised unemployment.",
        "storyboard": [
          {
            "frame_id": 1,
            "title": "The Three Economic Sectors",
            "description": "Following cotton from farm harvest (Primary) to fabric weaving (Secondary) to retail shop sale (Tertiary).",
            "key_takeaway": "Sectors are highly interdependent."
          },
          {
            "frame_id": 2,
            "title": "Disguised Unemployment in Farming",
            "description": "Depicting Laxmi's family of 5 working a 2-hectare plot where 2 members produce zero marginal yield.",
            "key_takeaway": "Underemployment hides surplus labor in agriculture."
          }
        ],
        "video_embeds": [
          {
            "video_id": "vid_ch2_01",
            "title": "Organised vs Unorganised Sectors & MGNREGA",
            "duration": "05:15",
            "video_url": "media/video_ch2_sectors.mp4",
            "transcript_summary": "Contrasts Kanta's formal job benefits with Kamal's informal vulnerability, explaining MGNREGA 2005 legal guarantees."
          }
        ],
        "hotspot_overlays": {
          "image_url": "ch2_sectors.png",
          "aspect_ratio": "16:9",
          "hotspots": [
            {
              "id": "h2_1",
              "x_percent": 5.0,
              "y_percent": 22.0,
              "w_percent": 40.0,
              "h_percent": 32.0,
              "popover_title": "Three Sectors & Interdependence",
              "popover_content": "Primary extracts natural goods, Secondary manufactures processed items, Tertiary provides transport, banking, and trade services."
            }
          ]
        },
        "key_terms": [
          "Primary Sector",
          "Secondary Sector",
          "Tertiary Sector",
          "Gross Domestic Product",
          "Disguised Unemployment",
          "Organised Sector",
          "Unorganised Sector",
          "MGNREGA 2005"
        ],
        "key_takeaways": [
          "GDP includes final goods only to avoid double counting.",
          "Primary sector retains excess labor with low GDP contribution.",
          "MGNREGA 2005 guarantees 100 days of rural wage employment.",
          "Public sector provides essential infrastructure driven by social welfare."
        ]
      },
      {
        "chapter_number": 3,
        "chapter_title": "Money and Credit",
        "executive_summary": "Money eliminates barter friction by acting as a universal medium of exchange. Commercial banks intermediate between depositors and borrowers, while credit can yield virtuous growth or vicious debt traps.",
        "storyboard": [
          {
            "frame_id": 1,
            "title": "Barter vs Modern Currency",
            "description": "Comparing double coincidence barter struggles with RBI-backed currency and demand deposit cheque transactions.",
            "key_takeaway": "Money acts as an accepted intermediate payment tool."
          }
        ],
        "video_embeds": [
          {
            "video_id": "vid_ch3_01",
            "title": "Formal Banking & Self-Help Groups (SHGs)",
            "duration": "04:30",
